=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist. The helpdesk ticket claims that
leaf_router_0_1 lost both its spine-facing uplinks and is isolated from the
Clos fabric, causing pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to be unable to reach pc_0_1
(10.0.1.2). Direct inspection of every device in the dc_clos_bgp topology
contradicts this entirely.

Both uplink interfaces on leaf_router_0_1 are administratively and operationally
UP: eth0 (172.16.0.7/31, connected to spine_router_0_0) and eth1
(172.16.0.11/31, connected to spine_router_0_1). The BGP sessions on both links
are fully Established, each receiving one prefix, and the BGP route to
10.0.0.0/24 is installed in leaf_router_0_1's forwarding table. Symmetrically,
leaf_router_0_0's uplinks are UP and its BGP route to 10.0.1.0/24 via
spine_router_0_0 is installed. Both spine routers and the super-spine carry all
expected BGP sessions (3/3 and 2/2 respectively), all Established with correct
prefix counts. An end-to-end ICMP probe from pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 completes with
zero packet loss and ~1 ms round-trip time.

Root cause: There is no fault. The ticket describes a scenario (both leaf
uplinks down) that is not present in the live lab; the topology is fully
converged and end-to-end reachability is intact.

Corrective action: None required. The team should verify whether the alert was
triggered against a stale or incorrect device state snapshot. No interface
restoration or BGP reconfiguration is needed.
=== END ===
